hypotheses.statement:
The tensor $B$ and the static lapse satisfy $$\begin{gathered}
 B=B_s\,\mathrm{d}s^2+B_t r^2\sigma,\qquad
 B_s=1-\frac{2m_0}{r^3},\qquad
 B_t=1+\frac{m_0}{r^3},\label{eq:B-components}\\
 \nabla^2N=NB,\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_{g_0}B=3,\qquad \mathop{\mathrm{div}}_{g_0}B=0,\qquad
 LN=0.\label{eq:static-identities}
\end{gathered}$$ Moreover $D$ is positive on all spherical harmonics and $$\label{eq:boundary-constants}
 b_*(4\pi a^2)=m_0,\qquad
 b_*'(4\pi a^2)=\frac{\kappa}{8\pi}.$$

For every smooth finite angular type seed in $C^{1,\alpha}_\tau$, the coefficients through degree two are well defined and $$u_j=r^{-3}\zeta_j(x)+o(r^{-3}),\qquad j=1,2.$$ The first coefficient has this asymptotic with arbitrarily many spatial derivatives. The second has it with the first spatial derivatives, in particular with every derivative needed at degree two below.*


*For the fourth-order construction, suppose specifically that $$\begin{gathered}
 q=\mathop{\mathrm{Hess}}v-Bv,\qquad Lv=0,\qquad v\longrightarrow0,\\
 v|_S\text{ has only constant and degree-one spherical modes}.
 \end{gathered}
 \label{ana:kernel-seed}$$ Corollary <a href="#cor:kernel" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="cor:kernel">16</a> will show that every seed with zero quadratic deficit has this form, so fourth-order estimates are needed only in this class. Then $q$ and all its derivatives have $O(r^{-3})$ scaled components. The coefficients $u_1,\ldots,u_4$ exist and have the above asymptotic with arbitrarily many spatial derivatives.*


*Let $p=2$ in the first case and $p=4$ in the second. In the coordinate $\eta=\operatorname{arsinh}r$, the conformal metric jet $G=(1+\sum_{j=1}^p\epsilon^ju_j)^4g_0$, taken through degree $p$, satisfies $$\begin{gathered}
 G-b=r^{-3}\bigl(C\,\mathrm{d}\eta^2+E r^2\sigma\bigr)+o(r^{-3}),\\
 C=2m_0+4\sum_{j=1}^p\epsilon^j\zeta_j,\qquad
 E=4\sum_{j=1}^p\epsilon^j\zeta_j.
 \end{gathered}
 \label{ana:metric-asymptotic}$$ For coefficient $j$, the remainder has this decay after angular and $\eta$ derivatives through order $p-j+1$, in scaled coframes $\mathrm{d}\eta,r\,\mathrm{d}x$.

For every sufficiently small member of the actual branch, $$p_\mu(\epsilon)-p_\mu(0)
 =\frac1{2\pi}\left\{
   \int_S\bigl(\psi\partial_sW_\mu-W_\mu\partial_s\psi\bigr)\,\mathrm{d}A_{g_0}
   -\int_M\bigl(W_\mu L\psi-\psi LW_\mu\bigr)\,\mathrm{d}V_{g_0}
                 \right\}.
 \label{ana:green}$$ In particular the right-hand side is an absolutely convergent integral expression. More generally the linear functional $$\mathcal G_\mu(u,F,B)
 =\frac1{2\pi}\left\{
 \int_S(u\partial_sW_\mu-W_\mu B)\,\mathrm{d}A_{g_0}
 -\int_M(W_\mu F-uLW_\mu)\,\mathrm{d}V_{g_0}\right\}
 \label{ana:green-functional}$$ is bounded in the norms $\|u\|_\beta+\|F\|_{2\beta}+\|B\|_{C^0(S)}$. For finite angular type coefficient jets, this same functional computes the coefficient of their metric mass.

Let $p\geq1$, and let $G$ be a smooth Riemannian metric jet of order $p$, with base $G_0=g_0$, whose coefficients have finite angular type. Suppose that there are angular scalar jets $C,E$, with $C_0=2m_0$ and $E_0=0$, such that $$\label{cmc:metric-asymptotics}
 G-b=r^{-3}\bigl(C\,\mathrm{d}\eta^2+E r^2\sigma\bigr)+o(r^{-3}).$$ For the coefficient of degree $j$, $1\leq j\leq p$, suppose that the remainder satisfies this estimate after every mixed radial and angular derivative of total order at most $p-j+1$.*

*There is a smooth family of sphere jets $\Sigma_s$, $0\leq s<\infty$, given by graphs over $\{s\}\times S^2$, with the following properties:*

1.  *$\Sigma_s$ has constant outward mean curvature through order $p$, and $\Sigma_0$ is minimal through that order.*

2.  *The graph coefficients have finite angular type. The normal lapse $f$ for variation in $s$ has base value $f_0=1$.*



3.  *If $A(s)$ and $H(s)$ are the area and mean curvature jets, then the CMC Hawking mass $$\label{cmc:hawking-definition}
     m_H(\Sigma_s)=
     \sqrt{\frac{A(s)}{16\pi}}
     \left(1-\frac{(H(s)^2-4)A(s)}{16\pi}\right)$$ satisfies, coefficientwise, $$\label{cmc:hawking-limit}
     \lim_{s\to\infty}m_H(\Sigma_s)
     =m_{\mathrm{AH}}(G)
     :=\sqrt{p_0(G)^2-\sum_{i=1}^3p_i(G)^2}.$$ Here $p_\mu(G)$ is the coefficientwise flux <a href="#eq:mass-flux" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:mass-flux">[eq:mass-flux]</a>, and the square root is its formal branch with base value $m_0>0$.

The following identity holds through the order of the sphere and metric jets: $$\label{cmc:hawking-variation}
 \frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d}s}m_H
 =\sqrt{\frac A{16\pi}}\frac{HA}{8\pi}
 \left[
 \bar f\left\langle
      \frac{\mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_G+6+|\mathrm{II}^\circ|^2}{2}
       \right\rangle
 +\frac1{\bar f}\langle\delta f\,J\delta f\rangle
 \right].$$

Use the construction of Proposition <a href="#prop:cmc-jets" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:cmc-jets">10</a> through order $2l$, where $l\ge1$, and let $A_{\mathrm{leaf}}=A(0)$ be the area jet of its minimal first leaf. Suppose, as ambient and leafwise coefficient identities, respectively, that $$\label{cmc:leading-assumptions}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Scal}}_G+6=\epsilon^{2l}|k|_{g_0}^2+O(\epsilon^{2l+1}),
 \qquad
 \mathrm{II}^\circ=\epsilon^lU+O(\epsilon^{l+1}),
 \qquad
 \delta f=\epsilon^lF+O(\epsilon^{l+1}).$$ Here $k$ is a smooth tensor on $M$; the fields $U,F$ on the background spheres are identified with fields on $M$. Then every coefficient below degree $2l$ of $m_{\mathrm{AH}}(G)-b_*(A_{\mathrm{leaf}})$ vanishes, and $$\label{cmc:leading-integral}
 \begin{split}
 &\bigl[m_{\mathrm{AH}}(G)-b_*(A_{\mathrm{leaf}})\bigr]_{2l}\\
 &\quad=\frac1{16\pi}\int_0^\infty N(s)
   \int_{\{s\}\times S^2}
    \left(|k|^2+|U|^2+2FJ_s^0F\right)\mathrm{d}A_{g_0}\,\mathrm{d}s
 \ge0.
 \end{split}$$ All norms in this formula use the background metrics. The displayed iterated integral is finite. If it vanishes, then $k,U,F$ vanish for $s>0$, and also at the boundary by continuity.

For the changed slice of Lemma <a href="#lem:slice" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:slice">19</a> there is a sphere jet $S^\sharp$, based at $S$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 |S^\sharp|_{\widetilde g}&=|S|_{g_\epsilon}+O(\epsilon^5),
 \label{quart:null-area}\\
 H_{\widetilde g}(S^\sharp)
      &=\epsilon^2k_{ss}|_S+O(\epsilon^3).
 \label{quart:null-h}
\end{aligned}$$ Let $S_{\min}$ be the minimal Taylor leaf of the CMC construction for $\widetilde g$. Their height jets agree through degree one. If $$z=D^{-1}(k_{ss}|_S),
 \label{quart:z}$$ then the height of $S^\sharp$ minus that of $S_{\min}$ is $\epsilon^2z+O(\epsilon^3)$, and $$|S^\sharp|_{\widetilde g}-|S_{\min}|_{\widetilde g}
     =\tfrac12\epsilon^4\int_S zDz\,\mathrm{d}A_{g_0}+O(\epsilon^5).
 \label{quart:area-hessian}$$ These statements remain valid when some leading boundary displacements are negative.

